# Late notebook corrections after the adversarial raw-layout review.
# The first executed pass treated 128 words as 8x16; figure inspection falsified
# that assumption.  Replace the affected narrative/cells and rewrite the notebook.

cells[2].source = r"""## 1. Bind the analysis to the actual raw-data layout

The HF event table contains exactly 128 flat ADC words per HRD event. A naive
`128 = 8 × 16` reshape is **wrong** for this source. Direct inspection against
the repository's 18-sample acquisition contract shows channel starts at flat
indices

\[
0,18,36,54,72,90,108,126.
\]

Therefore the stored vector is a **128-word prefix of an 18-sample/channel
stream**: channels 0–6 are complete (7×18=126 words) and channel 7 contributes
only its first two words. The B physics readouts use channels 0,2,4,6, so B2,
B4, B6 and B8 each have a complete 18-sample waveform.

This is independently visible in the raw bytes: slicing at 16-word boundaries
creates deterministic channel-boundary steps, while 18-word boundaries restore
continuous per-channel traces. The hrdSorter field `n_samples=16` therefore
cannot be used as waveform-layout authority for these parquet bytes and is
retained only as a conflicting provenance diagnostic.
"""

cells[3].source = """inventory = R.discover_inputs(ctx)
display(pd.DataFrame({
    'field': ['raw sample rows','raw words/event','source samples/channel','complete channels in prefix',
              'trailing words','complete B readouts','sorter n_samples','18-sample contract'],
    'value': [inventory['raw_sample_rows'], inventory['raw_words_per_event'],
              inventory['source_samples_per_channel'], inventory['complete_channels_in_prefix'],
              inventory['trailing_words_after_complete_channels'], ','.join(inventory['complete_B_readouts']),
              str(inventory['sorter_n_samples_values']), inventory['legacy_18_sample_config_status']]
}))
display(pd.DataFrame(inventory['layout_boundary_audit']).T)
"""

cells[4].source = r"""## 2. Reconstruct B2/B4/B6/B8 from the raw 18-sample windows — before thresholding

The source-bound B map is

| stave | raw channel | flat ADC window |
|---|---:|---:|
| B2 | 0 | [0,18) |
| B4 | 2 | [36,54) |
| B6 | 4 | [72,90) |
| B8 | 6 | [108,126) |

Each B waveform is reconstructed from its full 18-sample window. For the
configured positive-going even B channels,

\[
b=\mathrm{median}(x_0,x_1,x_2,x_3),\qquad
A=\max_k(x_k-b).
\]

There is **no per-channel amplitude cut before the event is assembled**. Only
after B2/B4/B6/B8 have all been reconstructed do we form

\[
\Delta E_{\rm ADC}=A(B2),\qquad
E_{\rm ADC}=A(B4)+A(B6)+A(B8).
\]

The provisional DAQ event identity is `(run, EVENTNO)` (`daq-event-key/1`).
`event_idx` is used only to verify row alignment with the parquet/sorter product,
and `EVT` is retained as a wrap/collision diagnostic.
"""

cells[6].source = r"""## 3. Raw-waveform and reconstruction quality checks

Before PID interpretation we inspect the **correct 18-sample B traces**, baseline
drift, peak position, first-four versus last-four baseline sensitivity, and run
support. The first-four region is explicitly marked because overlap between a
pulse and the baseline window is a possible systematic.

The hrdSorter event rows are cross-checked for `(event_idx, EVENTNO)` alignment,
but its `n_samples=16` metadata is **not** used to define the waveform layout;
the raw flat-boundary audit above falsifies that interpretation for this HF
product.
"""

nb["cells"] = cells
nbf.write(nb, OUT)
print("Rewrote notebook with corrected 18-sample raw-layout contract:", OUT)
